{"park":"hawaii_volcanoes","fetched":"2026-09-07T00:19:26+00:00","attribution":{"landmarks":"© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL","summaries":"Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0","boundary":"iNaturalist place 7222"},"landmarks":[{"id":"node/2734299214","name":"Kīlauea","kind":"peak","lon":-155.27581,"lat":19.41386,"ele_m":1222.0,"wikidata":"Q188698","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kīlauea","tour":6,"summary":{"extract":"Kīlauea is an active shield volcano in the Hawaiian Islands. It is located along the southeastern shore of Hawaii Island. The volcano is between 210,000 and 280,000 years old and grew above sea level about 100,000 years ago. Since the islands were settled, it has been the most active of the five volcanoes that together form the island and among the most active volcanoes on Earth. The most recent eruption began in December 2024, with episodic lava fountains and flows continuing into 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C4%ABlauea","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/K%C4%ABlauea_summit_eruption_map_%28September_2021%29.png/960px-K%C4%ABlauea_summit_eruption_map_%28September_2021%29.png","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C4%ABlauea_summit_eruption_map_(September_2021).png","dist_m":35,"width":640,"height":494,"license":"Public domain","license_url":"","artist":"Hawaiian Volcano Observatory for USGS"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/K%C4%ABlauea_summit_eruption_-_September_30%2C_2021_%2896774%29.jpg/960px-K%C4%ABlauea_summit_eruption_-_September_30%2C_2021_%2896774%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C4%ABlauea_summit_eruption_-_September_30,_2021_(96774).jpg","dist_m":207,"width":640,"height":960,"license":"Public domain","license_url":"","artist":"USGS Hawaiian Volcano Observatory"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/100_Years_at_Kilauea_-_NASA_Earth_Observatory.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:100_Years_at_Kilauea_-_NASA_Earth_Observatory.jpg","dist_m":300,"width":640,"height":857,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"NASA's Earth Observatory"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Kilauea_Crater_062.jpg/960px-Kilauea_Crater_062.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilauea_Crater_062.jpg","dist_m":384,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","artist":"Gillfoto"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Kilauea_Crater_066.jpg/960px-Kilauea_Crater_066.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilauea_Crater_066.jpg","dist_m":384,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","artist":"Gillfoto"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Smoking_Kilauea_Crater.JPG/960px-Smoking_Kilauea_Crater.JPG","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smoking_Kilauea_Crater.JPG","dist_m":384,"width":640,"height":427,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","artist":"RonPaul86"}],"street":null},{"id":"node/2189457641","name":"Mauna Loa","kind":"peak","lon":-155.6057,"lat":19.47553,"ele_m":4169.0,"wikidata":"Q159762","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauna_Loa","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Mauna Loa is one of five volcanoes that form the Island of Hawaii in the U.S. state of Hawaii in the Pacific Ocean. Mauna Loa is Earth's largest active volcano by both mass and volume. It was historically considered to be the largest volcano on Earth until the submarine mountain Tamu Massif was discovered to be larger. Mauna Loa is a shield volcano with relatively gentle slopes, and a volume estimated at 18,000 cubic miles (75,000 km3), although its peak is about 125 feet (38 m) lower than that of its neighbor, Mauna Kea. Lava eruptions from Mauna Loa are silica-poor and very fluid, and tend to be non-explosive.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauna_Loa","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/2734299272","name":"Mauna Ulu","kind":"peak","lon":-155.20157,"lat":19.36733,"ele_m":1053.0,"wikidata":"Q3300188","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauna_Ulu","tour":7,"summary":{"extract":"Maunaulu, also known as Mauna Ulu, is a volcanic cone in the eastern rift zone of the Kīlauea volcano on the island of Hawaiʻi. It falls within the bounds of Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park. Maunaulu was in a state of eruption from May 1969 to July 1974.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maunaulu","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Mauna_Ulu_and_Lava_Flow._Hawaii_Volcanoes_National_Park_%28504012%29_%2822248207666%29.jpg/960px-Mauna_Ulu_and_Lava_Flow._Hawaii_Volcanoes_National_Park_%28504012%29_%2822248207666%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mauna_Ulu_and_Lava_Flow._Hawaii_Volcanoes_National_Park_(504012)_(22248207666).jpg","dist_m":61,"width":640,"height":360,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"Robert Linsdell from St. Andrews, Canada"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/HAVO_20190523_Hiker_Mauna_Ulu_spatter_ramparts_JWei_%2847925230801%29.jpg/960px-HAVO_20190523_Hiker_Mauna_Ulu_spatter_ramparts_JWei_%2847925230801%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HAVO_20190523_Hiker_Mauna_Ulu_spatter_ramparts_JWei_(47925230801).jpg","dist_m":106,"width":640,"height":427,"license":"Public domain","license_url":"","artist":"Hawai‘i Volcanoes NPS"}],"street":null},{"id":"node/7549575706","name":"Puʻuʻokeʻokeʻo","kind":"peak","lon":-155.74184,"lat":19.21361,"ele_m":2096.0,"wikidata":"Q7257197","url":"https://